5. Contour Fusion
    5.1. Setup
        5.1.1. Load fusion parameters
        5.1.2. Load per-subject boundary graphs
        5.1.3. Build seeds and expected region IDs
            5.1.3.1. Masked to chosen hemisphere
            5.1.3.2. Compute per-region seeds
            5.1.3.3. Compile list of non-zero region IDs present per-slice
    5.2. Constructing per-subject line network
        5.2.1. Normalize weights
        5.2.2. Decompose per-slice graph into arcs + nodes (first pass)
            5.2.2.1. Find arc endpoints within tolerance -> node junctions
            5.2.2.2. Record closed island loops (no nodes)
        5.2.3. Inject phantom regions
            5.2.3.1. Template built for every symmetrically different region R
                5.2.3.1.1. Bounding arcs
                5.2.3.1.2. Neighbor codes
                5.2.3.1.3. Boundary junctions
                5.2.3.1.4. Junction structure (topology)
            5.2.3.2. Reduce region R for addition to other subject(s)
                5.2.3.2.1. Periphery -> reduces to line
                    5.2.3.2.1.1. Project R onto other subject(s)
                    5.2.3.2.1.2. Cut shared arcs where they intersect with R, place new nodes (leave internal arcs alone)
                    5.2.3.2.1.3. Collapse R’s boundary to that of the shared boundary between new nodes
                5.2.3.2.2. Enclosed (<= 1 neighbor) -> reduces to point
                    5.2.3.2.2.1. Collapse R’s boundary to centroid/seed point
                5.2.3.2.3. Enclosed (>= 2 neighbor, meets at one point w/o R, every R-junction is a triple point) -> reduced to clustered, superimposed points
                    5.2.3.2.3.1. Specially mark each arc (wall) of R
                    5.2.3.2.3.2. Collapse R’s boundary to a cluster of triple points, each connected to two of the shared regions, and connected to each other by zero-length arcs.
                5.2.3.2.4. Other (complex) cases -> Halts contour fusion for that slice (failure mode)
                    5.2.3.2.4.1. Records location of failure
            5.2.3.3. Output records of added phantom regions and complex cases
        5.2.4. Decompose updated per-slice graph into arcs + nodes (second pass)
            5.2.4.1. Find arc endpoints within tolerance -> node junctions
            5.2.4.2. Record closed island loops (no nodes)
            5.2.4.3. Phantom regions loudly declared
    5.3. Node fusion
        5.3.1. Match node junctions across subjects
            5.3.1.1. Match through incident region sets
            5.3.1.2. Collisions resolved by Hungarian assessment within a set displacement
                5.3.1.2.1. Phantom node junctions exempt from displacement requirement
            5.3.1.3. Averaging step
        5.3.2. End node fusion per-slice if any of the following occurs
            5.3.2.1. Any complex (non-autoresolvable) cases are present
            5.3.2.2. Node match rate is below a certain threshold
            5.3.2.3. There are any other conflicting/unmatched node junctions
    5.4. Per-arc fusion (Edge fusion)
        5.4.1. Order subjects and incorporate weights
            5.4.1.1. Define reference subject (manual toggle, subject with most nodes, then least phantom regions, then first subject)
            5.4.1.2. Starting with reference subject, pull other subjects’ arcs and weights into shared space
                5.4.1.2.1. Renormalize weights per-arc
        5.4.2. Orient arcs per-subject to establish point-wise correspondence
            5.4.2.1. For island regions -> resample all copies to a common point count, force consistent winding (using signed area), and rotate (using FFT correlation) until start points line up
            5.4.2.2. For arcs -> force all arcs to run in the same direction as the reference
                5.4.2.2.1. Evaluate difference between forward and reverse orientations of each arc
        5.4.3. Determine curvature per-point for every arc polyline
            5.4.3.1. Every arc polyline gets an ArcRep wrapper
                5.4.3.1.1. Stores working dense copy of arc internally
        5.4.4. Create anchor table per-arc (keypoints = True)
            5.4.4.1. Determine keypoints
                5.4.4.1.1. Walk each arc’s curvature to find local maxima of |curvature| above a certain floor within a certain interval
            5.4.4.2. Match keypoints
                5.4.4.2.1. Use a benefit matrix (two points must be close and at a similar point in the arc) to propose assignments
                5.4.4.2.2. Solve matching with Hungarian algorithm
            5.4.4.3. Add keypoints to anchor table
                5.4.4.3.1. Keypoints with 2+ matches get added
                5.4.4.3.2. Subjects missing an anchor get a replacement via linear interpolation between existing anchors
        5.4.5. Create anchor table per-arc (keypoints = False)
            5.4.5.1. Anchor table only returns [0, 1] (endpoints)
        5.4.6. Piecewise fusion between consecutive anchors
            5.4.6.1. Determine # of sample points between anchor intervals
                5.4.6.1.1. Take sagitta bound, resampling and growing until within tolerance or above max samples permitted
                    5.4.6.1.1.1. Label “saturated” if the bound is outside tolerance even with max samples.
                5.4.6.1.2. Output max chords required among subjects
            5.4.6.2. Resample each subject’s segment with # of sample points
                5.4.6.2.1. Place points by arc length (lambda = 0)
                5.4.6.2.2. Place points by curvature (lambda = 1)
                5.4.6.2.3. Place points by a blend of both methods (0 < lambda < 1)
            5.4.6.3. Take weighted pointwise mean of all sample points
        5.4.7. Concatenate fused per-sample segments into arc polylines
            5.4.7.1. Drop duplicate anchors to avoid double counting
        5.4.8. Snap arc endpoints to fused nodes
            5.4.8.1. Distribute required correction linearly along the arc
            5.4.8.2. Identical points are merged
    5.5. Polygon construction
        5.5.1. Prepare arcs
            5.5.1.1. Drop degenerate arcs
            5.5.1.2. Remove duplicate geometry
            5.5.1.3. (optional) Snap dangling ends to nodes within tolerance
        5.5.2. Polygonize arcs
            5.5.2.1. Count interior arc crossings
            5.5.2.2. Split arcs at crossings
            5.5.2.3. Produce faces using polygonize
                5.5.2.3.1. If error arises, try un-noded, then rebuild one ring per region (won’t represent holes, not ideal)
        5.5.3. Assign faces to regions
            5.5.3.1. A face bounded by arcs takes the common region ID
            5.5.3.2. Neighbor rule is used to resolve island / donut pairs
            5.5.3.3. Use distance-transform seed to decide ambiguities
            5.5.3.4. Everything else is given an UNLABELED designation and RID
        5.5.4. Update per-slice registry
        5.5.5. Rebuild per-subject atlases (for diagnostics if enabled)
        5.5.6. Output polygons as atlas
6. Conversion to volumetric model
    6.1. Mirror Hemisphere back for Bilateral Symmetry
        6.1.1. Reflect kept-hemisphere graph across the L-R midline
        6.1.2. Weld the reflection to the original at the midline
            6.1.2.1. Merge coincident nodes along the midline seam
            6.1.2.2. Drop duplicated midline arcs so that faces can merge across the center
        6.1.3. Rebuild full-brain faces from the welded graph
        6.1.4. Ensure same region IDs on both hemispheres
    6.2. Rasterize polygonized atlas into a labeled volume
        6.2.1. Load template label volume for grid dimensions and affine
        6.2.2. Allocate an empty int16 volume, set background ID to zero
        6.2.3. Per-Slice Work
            6.2.3.1. Merges every multi-polygon into single list of polygons
            6.2.3.2. Sort each polygon face by area in descending order
            6.2.3.3. Fill each face's exterior ring with its region ID (using even-odd rule)
            6.2.3.4. Sets interior/enclosing regions to zero
            6.2.3.5. Nested regions painted later overwrite the enclosing region
            6.2.3.6. Write each slice into the label volume
                6.2.3.6.1. No reorientation required
                6.2.3.6.2. Coordinate Swap
        6.2.4. Skip slices with no faces
        6.2.5. Verify orientation on a representative slice (overlay vs template, IoU)
        6.2.6. Unresolved faces carry unlabeled IDs into the volume as a stray material
    6.3. Write the Label Volume in M3C’s input format (SPR/SDT)
        6.3.1. Assert that the in-plane grid as a square
        6.3.2. Build the header
            6.3.2.1. Dim - the grid size
            6.3.2.2. Interval - voxel size
            6.3.2.3. FOV/field of view
        6.3.3. Binary (.sdt)
        6.3.4. Copy to local disk instead of reading across Google Drive before handing to M3C
    6.4. Multiple-Material Marching Cubes
        6.4.1. Build for host-platform (python compatible)
        6.4.2. Read SPR/SDT
        6.4.3. Set M3C Parameters
            6.4.3.1. Slice Range (all slices or a sub-range)
            6.4.3.2. Cap (close each surface with a flat lid at the boundary of each slice range)
            6.4.3.3. Linearize
            6.4.3.4. Centering around the origin
            6.4.3.5. X-Y-Z spacing
        6.4.4. March a 2x2x2 voxel cube through the volume
            6.4.4.1. Classify the 8 corners by Region ID
            6.4.4.2. Emit triangles from case table
            6.4.4.3. Walls shared between 2 regions are generated once to prevent gaps/overlaps
        6.4.5. Place vertices at the midpoints of edges
            6.4.5.1. No interpolation possible between region IDs
            6.4.5.2. Produces a staircase lattice
        6.4.6. Output Generation
            6.4.6.1. One PolyData surface (.vtk) per material
            6.4.6.2. Combined node/element files
    6.5. Surface Conditioning
        6.5.1. Read VTK Files
        6.5.2. Taubin Smoothing (preserves volume, unlike traditional Laplacian Smoothing)
    6.6. Output Deliverables
        6.6.1. NIfTI Label-Map on the template affine
        6.6.2. Region Look-up Table (CSV)
        6.6.3. ITK-SNAP Label Description File
        6.6.4. FreeSurfer Look-up Table (for 3D Slicer)
        6.6.5. Surface
            6.6.5.1. Per-region STL and OBJ
            6.6.5.2. Per-region VTK PolyData for 3D Slicer
        6.6.6. Merge all regions into one interactive mesh
